Workstream ID: atlas-living-motion
Title: Living atmosphere and expressive motion for Discovery Atlas
Status: DONE
Milestone: Make the Atlas visibly lively while keeping real work and user control clear
Dependencies: atlas-progression-redesign, discovery-atlas, appearance-and-identity
GitHub issue: https://github.com/Zanark/CareerOS/issues/25
Purpose: Respond to the owner's request for a much more animated, inviting Atlas.
  Add original chapter-specific atmosphere, constellation motion and expressive
  interactions without changing saved work, rewards or completion authority.

Tasks:
  [x] Inspect the existing local Atlas and choose a coherent motion language.
  [x] Add distinct ambient atmosphere to all seven chapter scenes.
  [x] Animate the constellation, reward invitation and collection interactions.
  [x] Add a visible pause control and honor system reduced motion.
  [x] Suspend ambient work offscreen/hidden and preserve event-driven WebGL.
  [x] Qualify animation, controls, layouts, tutorial and data/resource boundaries.
  [x] Update affected guides, presentation policy and owned continuity.
  [x] Synchronize this workstream and verify its original issue/Project item.

Acceptance criteria:
  Default motion is visibly richer than the preceding local prototype, with
    distinct authored chapter effects rather than a uniform blinking treatment.
  Decorative motion cannot earn EXP, reveal a discovery as owned, complete a
    checkpoint or change records, mission focus, schema, backups or journal data.
  Discovered remains the default; existing expeditions, previews and records work.
  Pause is visible and keyboard-operable; it does not trap focus or prevent work.
  System reduced motion stays authoritative, including changes while open.
  Reading content/counters remain stable; no flashing, shake or autoplay audio.
  Ongoing decoration pauses offscreen and in hidden documents; no idle
    JavaScript/WebGL render loop or extra 3D context is introduced.
  Native render-budget, orbit/pan/zoom/reset, fallback and disposal still work.
  Both themes, desktop/tablet/narrow layouts and tutorial isolation are covered
    using disposable fictional contexts, never the owner's browser profile.
  The earlier finite-only presentation rule is deliberately revised for
    controllable visible CSS/SVG ambience, not silently confused with WebGL.
  Technical motion/layout checks do not claim human aesthetic or motivational
    approval. Existing image-review limits remain respected.
  This is local implementation, not source commit/push/deployment authorization.

